dict(d=D, c="ivx mackinnon obsessive interview", m="say", l=2,
q="An obsessive engineer answers every question with exhaustive detail, corrects your word choices, and describes his divorce without any visible feeling. Following MacKinnon and Michels, which approach is most helpful?",
a="Avoid power struggles over details and gently draw attention to feelings, for example noting what he seems to leave out",
ae="MacKinnon describes obsessive patients as using intellectualization, isolation of affect and control. The interviewer avoids battles over accuracy or detail, which the patient will win, and instead points gently toward affect: “You’ve told me a great deal about the logistics of the divorce, but not much about how it felt.”",
w=[("Match his precision with equally detailed questions to build rapport",
"Matching the detail feeds the defense and turns the interview into a technical exchange, avoiding feeling altogether."),
("Confront his lack of emotion directly as a defense against grief",
"Direct confrontation early often triggers more control and intellectualization. Gentle observation is more effective."),
("Let him control the interview completely to avoid upsetting him",
"Ceding control entirely leads to a long, affectless interview. The interviewer must retain leadership without fighting.")],
x="MacKinnon also notes that obsessive patients fear losing control and being shamed by error. They respond well to clear structure and respect for their competence, combined with patient, repeated attention to the feelings they isolate. Humor, used carefully, can sometimes loosen the defense.",
s="MacKinnon, Michels & Buckley, The Psychiatric Interview in Clinical Practice (3rd ed.)",
h="Obsessive patient: “Don’t argue the decimal; ask about the heart.”"),

